 ## Overview
-LU-NeRF (Local Unposed NeRF) is a method for estimating camera poses and reconstructing neural scene representations from unordered, unposed image collections, specifically designed for few-shot scenarios [S1]. It differs from prior unposed NeRF approaches by operating in the general SE(3) pose setting without restrictive pose priors, utilizing a local-to-global pipeline that first learns local mini-scene representations and then synchronizes them into a global frame [S1]. Unlike methods that rely on coarse-to-fine frequency scheduling like BARF, LU-NeRF employs a compact MLP without positional encoding and resolves mirror-symmetry ambiguities through a dedicated secondary training stage [S1].
+LU-NeRF estimates camera poses and neural scene representations from unordered, unposed image collections by processing them as local mini-scenes before global synchronization [S1]. It differs from prior unposed NeRF methods by operating in the general SE(3) pose setting without restrictive pose priors, using a compact view-independent 4-layer MLP without positional encoding, and resolving mirror-symmetry ambiguities through a dedicated secondary training stage [S1].
 
 ## How it works
-**Inputs:** A set of unposed images partitioned into mini-scenes.
-**Outputs:** Optimized camera poses (world-to-camera) and a neural radiance field representation for each mini-scene.
+**Inputs:** A set of unposed images partitioned into mini-scenes [S1].
+**Outputs:** Optimized camera poses (world-to-camera) and a neural radiance field representation for each mini-scene [S1].
 
